const unit2Deck = [

  {"tag": "2.1", "question": "What does organizing the project work define, and how is it framed from a systems perspective? What inputs (elements at our disposal) does it start from?", "answer": "Organizing project work is the first activity after the official approval and commencement of a project. It defines how work is distributed between project activities, the sequence of activities, their outputs as deliverables and milestones, and how the product or service will be tested for conformity to user requirements. Systems perspective: input (available information) → tools and techniques → output. Inputs (mnemonic ‘Can Peter Pack Everything?’): the project charter (objective and high-level requirements); preliminary estimates like costs, risk and communication from similar projects and the feasibility study; processes, procedures and standards required by the PMO or organization; plus environmental factors: laws, regulations and standards, the culture and structure of the organization and its operational environment, and available technology and human-resource support. The PM and initial team apply expert judgment and methodologies to determine what is required within the project constraints and stakeholder expectations. Result: a detailed plan of project activities including material and human resources; alternative plans for dealing with change and in anticipation of risk are also considered."},
  {"tag": "2.1", "question": "Why are baselines needed when organizing project work, which subsidiary plans make up the overall planning, and what additional considerations are included?", "answer": "Project baselines for scope, schedule and cost are developed as a reference frame for comparing and benchmarking progress (Herroelen & Leus, 2004). The overall planning consists of subsidiary plans for managing (mnemonic: triangle + three groups): scope, schedule, cost | requirements, quality, process improvement | human resources, communications, stakeholders | procurement, risk (11 plans). Additional considerations: the life cycle approach of each phase, how work will be executed, project management processes including reviews and auditing, implementation details, the tools and techniques for developing the product or service, and configuration management."},
  {"tag": "2.1", "question": "What is a work breakdown structure (WBS) and why is it needed?", "answer": "Visualizing the work of a project can be overwhelming, especially in large IT projects. Visualization starts by breaking the work into manageable components that can be completed independently, in sequence or in parallel. This is the work breakdown structure (WBS): the subdivision of project work and deliverables into smaller and more manageable components."},
  {"tag": "2.1", "question": "MCQ (IU unit test): How does a WBS help project planning?\nA) It imposes a hierarchy of project activities.\nB) It provides a breakdown of the project product or service.\nC) It indicates stakeholder responsibilities.\nD) It provides an ordering of project activities.", "answer": "B (confirmed IU key). A WBS breaks down the project product or service (work and deliverables). Ordering activities = precedence/PERT/Gantt (2.3); stakeholder responsibilities = RACI (4.2); WBS boxes are deliverables, not activities."},
  {"tag": "2.1", "question": "Describe Figure 8 (Work Breakdown Structure) of the cRobot project: what each of the four levels represents, with examples.", "answer": "Example project: a robot that follows its owners around the house carrying their coffee mug (cRobot). Mnemonic ‘Please Pass Five Tests’ = Project → Phases → Functions → Tasks. Level 1: the general scope (cRobot), the root of the decomposition tree and most abstract view of the project. Level 2: initial breakdown into general phases: requirements definition, design specifications, testing. Level 3: functional entities: functional / engineering / IT requirements; hardware / software design; hardware / software / integration test. Level 4: specific tasks each Level 3 unit breaks into, e.g. behavior definitions, autonomy characteristics, safety features; mobility, stress tolerance, independence, communication; processing power, signaling, artificial intelligence, sensor input; sensors, actuators, robot body, on-board computer; signal processing, audio, NLP, movement, comms; sensors/actuators/stress/computer test; unit/interface/system tests; mobility/comm/interaction/field test. Level 4 is where the project outcome takes shape in terms of appearance and usability. Figure labels: dashed line ‘Project’ under Level 1, ‘WPs’ under Level 2, ‘Tasks’ under Level 3."},
  {"tag": "2.1", "question": "Which WBS levels form the work packages and which form the tasks? How are schedule, cost and resources determined, and when is the WBS finalized?", "answer": "Work packages = the Level 2 units and often some core Level 3 units. Individual tasks (the body of the project) = the Level 4 units and occasionally some Level 3 units. Schedule, cost and resources are identified for each Level 4 element; their aggregates feed upward to form the schedule, cost and resources of Level 3, and so on up to Level 1. If the totals are within the allowed project constraints, the WBS is finalized; otherwise the process is repeated until a satisfactory WBS is achieved or the project requirements are updated to ensure feasibility. The WP and task levels are where partner distribution takes place and contractual agreements are drawn. (tutor-only, CONFLICT) The tutor calls the lowest WBS level the ‘work package’ and says WBS boxes are written as nouns (deliverables), with activities defined in a separate document; in the exam use the book: WPs = Level 2 (+ core Level 3), tasks = Level 4."},
  {"tag": "2.1", "question": "How do deliverables, milestones and unique identifiers relate to the WBS, and how does WBS use vary with project size?", "answer": "The final WBS level is where work is shaped into the deliverables to be completed. Almost all Level 4 work elements result in a deliverable. Milestones signify important achievements and transitions into project phases and are reserved for critical points in the life cycle; in Figure 8, Level 2 (and occasionally select Level 3) elements are separated by milestones. Unique identifiers are established for each task and work package. The cRobot example is typical of a large and complex project; the number and naming of levels vary by type and size; smaller projects use fewer levels and maybe no explicit WBS at all."},
  {"tag": "2.1", "question": "How is work broken down in agile development? Define epic, user story, task and sprint.", "answer": "Agile uses different concepts: epics → user stories → tasks. Epic (PMBOK 2013, p. 5): ‘a high-level or complex user story to be refined into more detailed user stories.’ User story (PMBOK 2013, p. 6): ‘a narrative description of a software requirement, function, feature, or quality attribute, presented as a narrative of desired user interactions with a software system.’ Task: a small package of work that can be completed within one iteration, called a sprint in Scrum. Glossary – Sprint: ‘a defined period of time to work on a certain element of the project to move the process forward within a Scrum management process.’"},
  {"tag": "2.1", "question": "What is a traceability matrix and why is it used?", "answer": "A grid that links product or service requirements with the work or deliverables that satisfy them, documenting the relationship between requirements and work performed. Purposes: ensures each requirement adds business value by linking it to the business and project objectives (Kirova, Kirby, Kothari, & Childress, 2008); verifies all requirements are addressed at all levels of work (design, implementation, test) — important regardless of project size; ensures no more work is performed than required (e.g. test cases are always based on some requirement). It is not the only way: development environments sometimes provide similar functionality without a traceability matrix."},
  {"tag": "2.1", "question": "How does a traceability matrix work, what information does it contain, and describe Figure 9.", "answer": "It crosses the WBS horizontally, following requirements from beginning to end, where the output is tested and approved. It traces business needs as expressed by their functional requirements, the status of related activities, and the changes to each activity as the project progresses. It also contains technical specifications, system components affected, software modules developed or adopted from an existing solution, and the test cases confirming successful completion and their status. Details are kept in a dedicated design document (or folder). Benefit: helps detect the impact of project deviations on the deliverables and guide alternative ways to complete the work. Figure 9 (Requirements traceability matrix): header with project name, project owner, project manager, project description; columns ID, Assoc. ID (e.g. 1 → 1.1, 1.2, 1.3), then (mnemonic ‘Five Dogs Took Seven Sausages To Sam’s Car’) Functional requirement, Design document, Technical specs, System components, Software module, Test case, Status, Comments."},
  {"tag": "2.1", "question": "MCQ: Which of the following is NOT a purpose of a traceability matrix according to the coursebook?\nA) Ensuring each requirement adds business value\nB) Ensuring no more work is performed than required\nC) Assigning responsibilities to stakeholders\nD) Verifying requirements are addressed in design, implementation and test", "answer": "C. Assigning stakeholder responsibilities is the RACI matrix (4.2). A traceability matrix links requirements to the work and deliverables satisfying them."},
  {"tag": "2.1", "question": "In what document is the WBS materialized, what does it contain, and what validation follows?", "answer": "The WBS dictionary (or, in other cases, the project backlog). Contents (mnemonic ‘A Real Team Always Respects Quality’): activities with their schedule and deliverables; the responsible partner or group; the traceability matrix; assumptions and constraints; required resources and their associated costs; quality requirements and acceptance criteria. A validation process follows, which may include evaluation of the WBS by all partners and acceptance of its structure, to ensure everyone is on board and agrees on what it takes for the work to be successfully completed (Regan, Flood, & McCaffery, 2015)."},
  {"tag": "2.1", "question": "(tutor-only) What does the tutor add about the WBS and the requirements traceability matrix (RTM)?", "answer": "(tutor-only) A WBS typically has 3–4 levels; complex products go deeper (NASA uses up to 7). WBS boxes are nouns (deliverables/outcomes), not verbs (activities); activities under the lowest level are listed in another document and later sequenced into the schedule. Purposes of the WBS: communicating to all stakeholders what the project will deliver, and numbering that links each element to the finance system’s cost-centre IDs to track costs. The tutor says the WBS is used in waterfall/traditional projects, not agile. The RTM can be shared with the client for transparency, but this is not a must."},
  {"tag": "2.1", "question": "LONG ANSWER (18 points): Explain how project work is organized using a work breakdown structure. Describe its levels, how it is finalized, how it is documented, and the role of the traceability matrix. Contrast with agile.", "answer": "Organizing work is the first activity after official approval; it defines distribution of work, sequence, deliverables and milestones, and testing for conformity to user requirements. Systems view: input → tools and techniques → output. Inputs: charter, preliminary estimates (similar projects, feasibility study), PMO/organizational processes and standards, environmental factors (laws, regulations, standards; culture and structure; technology and HR support). Expert judgment produces a detailed plan with material and human resources and alternative plans for change and risk; baselines for scope, schedule and cost are the reference frame for benchmarking (Herroelen & Leus), within subsidiary plans (scope, schedule, cost, requirements, procurement, quality, process improvement, HR, risk, communications, stakeholders). WBS = subdivision of project work and deliverables into smaller, more manageable components that can be completed independently, in sequence or in parallel. cRobot (Figure 8): Level 1 project root (most abstract); Level 2 general phases (requirements definition, design specifications, testing); Level 3 functional entities; Level 4 specific tasks (where outcome takes shape in appearance and usability). WPs = Level 2 + core Level 3; tasks = Level 4 + some Level 3. Schedule, cost and resources identified at Level 4 aggregate upward; finalized if within constraints, else repeated or requirements updated. WP and task levels = partner distribution and contractual agreements. Almost all Level 4 elements produce deliverables; milestones (critical points, between Level 2 elements) mark achievements and phase transitions; unique identifiers for each task and WP. Levels vary with size; small projects may have no explicit WBS. Agile: epics → user stories → tasks completed within a sprint (PMBOK definitions of epic and user story). Traceability matrix: grid linking requirements to the work/deliverables satisfying them; ensures business value, all requirements addressed at all levels, no more work than required (test cases based on requirements); crosses the WBS horizontally from requirement to tested, approved output; tracks functional requirements, status, changes, technical specs, system components, software modules, test cases; detail in a design document; helps detect impact of deviations and guide alternatives. Documentation: WBS dictionary / project backlog (activities with schedule and deliverables, responsible partner, traceability matrix, assumptions and constraints, resources and costs, quality requirements and acceptance criteria), followed by validation and acceptance by all partners (Regan et al.)."},
  
  {"tag": "2.2", "question": "Why must requirements be prioritized, on what bases can prioritization be done, and why is it important?", "answer": "Once the requirements of the project product or service have been identified, they must be prioritized as a basis for deciding on the order in which to perform the work. Prioritization can be based on risk, funding, and other considerations relevant to the strategic objectives of a project and in relation to other ongoing projects in the organization (mnemonic ‘Real Friends Share Others’). Importance: in addition to its primary function of organizing the sequence of project work and identifying milestones, it allows project monitoring and alerts to task breakdowns and priority shifts. Activities with high priority draw the project team’s attention, and resources are allocated to ensure their successful completion on time and on budget (Larson & Gray, 2015). After identifying the elementary tasks and grouping them into functional activities, they are prioritized by their significance to the project outcome, according to their urgency or their importance."},
  {"tag": "2.2", "question": "Contrast urgent activities with important activities: response mode, effects, advantages and drawbacks.", "answer": "Urgent activities or crises require immediate attention and focus to alleviate their pressure on the rest of the activities and the outcome (Carneiro, Silva, & Alencar, 2018). The response mode is typically reactive and puts the team in a defensive and narrowly focused mindset as they are pressured to deliver on time; this might trigger a knee-jerk response that is not always optimal and may jeopardize project success. Important activities ensure the long-term goals of the project; they allow the team to work in a proactive mode that affords rational decision-making and openness to new opportunities. Prioritizing important tasks can get the job done and potentially deter future crisis situations, but it suffers from the subjective evaluation of what is important and the many aspects of why something may be important, e.g. importance for the project sponsor or the project manager’s career."},
  {"tag": "2.2", "question": "MCQ (IU unit test): What is the risk of high-priority activities?\nA) They increase the probability of project failure.\nB) They draw the attention of stakeholders.\nC) They can become the focus of the team, leading to neglect of other issues and important activities.\nD) They can drain project resources.", "answer": "C (confirmed IU key). Related Mastersolution key: the risk of defining high-priority activities is that ‘they risk putting the team in a defensive and narrowly-focused mindset.’ The book says high-priority activities draw the team’s (not stakeholders’) attention, and resources are allocated (not drained) to complete them on time and on budget."},
  {"tag": "2.2", "question": "Describe the urgency vs importance matrix (Figure 10): its source, the rule for urgent vs important tasks, and all four quadrants with their labels, actions and examples.", "answer": "A more balanced approach combines urgency and importance and considers their combined effect, arranged in a matrix (Covey, 2013), often called an Eisenhower matrix, with four quadrants along the two dimensions. Urgent tasks need immediate attention, so their priority is high; important tasks require ongoing attention and can be delayed unless an urgent task requires everyone’s attention. Quadrants (mnemonic ‘Fat Ants Avoid Lemons’ = Focus, Attend, Avoid, Limit): 1) High importance + high urgency = CRITICAL (Focus): critical for project success, a top priority; crisis situations qualify. 2) High importance + low urgency = GOALS (Attend): focus on the project goals through appropriate planning, searching for opportunities, productive collaborations, and development. 3) Low importance + high urgency = INTERRUPTIONS (Avoid): attention is required, but the whole activity could be avoided without any serious effect on the project; example: reporting project progress to external stakeholders. 4) Low importance + low urgency = DISTRACTIONS (Limit): a waste of time and resources; if they cannot be avoided, their effect should be limited; examples: overanalyzing a problem, micromanaging, gossiping. Figure 10 axes: urgency (x, low to high), importance (y, low to high)."},
  {"tag": "2.2", "question": "MCQ (IU unit test): Activities with low importance and low urgency are considered…\nA) low-focus.\nB) unnecessary.\nC) distractions.\nD) interruptions.", "answer": "C (confirmed IU key). Distractions (Limit). Interruptions (Avoid) is the high-urgency, low-importance quadrant. (tutor-only, CONFLICT) The tutor said distractions should be avoided completely; the figure says Limit and the text says their effect should be limited if they cannot be avoided. Use the book."},
  {"tag": "2.2", "question": "What is the Kano model: who developed it, for what purpose, which belief does it challenge, and how does it classify requirements?", "answer": "The Kano model (Lo, Shen, & Chen, 2017) was developed by Noraka Kano (the coursebook’s spelling) for quality control purposes in relation to customer satisfaction. Its purpose was to challenge the conventional belief that customer satisfaction is proportional to the improvement of every aspect of a product or service. Instead, improving certain aspects with a certain amount of effort can meet basic customer expectations, while improving others with the same effort can delight customers. Like most methodologies, it abstracts requirements into categories, allowing their identification through classifiers rather than by an arbitrary and often subjective attachment of attributes to requirements. It splits requirements into three, four, or five categories depending on the fine-grained version of the product or service under development; Figure 11 shows a three-category classification."},
  {"tag": "2.2", "question": "Describe the three Kano categories with the cRobot examples, their drawbacks, and the resulting priority of each. Describe Figure 11.", "answer": "Mnemonic ‘Bread, Sauce, Dessert’. 1) Basic (baseline expectations): requirements that must be present for a product or service to be successful; the absolute minimum customers expect, even though they may not mention them because from their view it was ‘obvious’. cRobot: mobility. It won’t impress customers, but if missing they will rightfully be dissatisfied. Must be met at any cost and typically needs proper quality testing. Priority: mandatory, high priority, even though customers often forget to mention them. 2) Linear satisfiers (performance needs): proportionally increase satisfaction (the more of them, the more satisfied); they include the standard functionality (Lin et al., 2017). cRobot: the faster the robot responds, the more satisfied the customer. Rewards are proportional to investment, but the effort also increases; at some point the return on investment begins to drop. Priority: lower, but should generally be implemented unless there is good reason not to. 3) Delighters: requirements that can attract, uplift, delight or ‘blow the mind’ of the customer. cRobot: human-like head movement as the robot moves. Customers do not expect or mention them beforehand, but they add a lot to overall impression and satisfaction; even small facets can raise the impression to premium levels. Drawbacks: may distract the team from core requirements; may change customer expectations and be considered basic when overdone; their positive impact declines over time as customers become familiar with them. Priority: implement a few, but not many, because of effort, cost and diminishing returns. Figure 11: satisfaction (y) against functionality (x); basic attributes = curve below the axis flattening towards it; performance attributes = straight line through the origin (satisfiers); attractive attributes = curve rising steeply (delighters)."},
  {"tag": "2.2", "question": "MCQ: In the Kano model, which category describes requirements whose positive impact declines over time as customers become familiar with them?\nA) Basic expectations\nB) Linear satisfiers\nC) Delighters\nD) Must-haves", "answer": "C. Delighters: they can also come to be considered basic when overdone, and may distract the team from core requirements."},
  {"tag": "2.2", "question": "What is MoSCoW: who developed it, when, why words instead of numbers, and what are its four categories with the cRobot examples?", "answer": "A prioritization technique often considered in project management. Must-haves (M), Should-haves (S), Could-haves (C), Won’t-haves (W); the Os are included for mnemonic convenience. Developed by Dai Clengg of Oracle UK in 1994 to identify the order of importance of requirements in software development. Words are used instead of numbers to widen the scope of each category and trigger a discussion of what is important (Kravchenko & Bruskin, 2017). Must-haves: the absolute minimum requirements necessary for meeting the business needs of the project output (cRobot: a skeleton structure). Should-haves: could enhance the product or service but are not necessary for its success (cRobot: a smooth, polished external surface that makes it aesthetically appealing so people buy it). Could-haves: don’t affect the project outcome but would be nice to have (cRobot: eyes and a mouth drawn on the chassis, or moving them to simulate humanlike emotional responses). INCONSISTENCY: the text also says ‘would-haves are future requirements that could be considered later’; the four categories remain must, should, could, won’t."},
  {"tag": "2.2", "question": "MCQ (IU unit test): What prioritization categories does MoSCoW consider?\nA) should-haves, could-haves, must-haves, and won’t-haves\nB) must-do, should-do, could-do, and won’t-do\nC) must-haves, should-haves, could-haves, and would-haves\nD) more, some, could, and won’t", "answer": "A (confirmed IU key). The order is scrambled but the four categories are right. C is the trap built on the book’s ‘would-haves’ slip."},
  {"tag": "2.2", "question": "What is the main problem with MoSCoW prioritization, with the coursebook’s example and its extreme consequence?", "answer": "The problem lies mostly with its application when different organizational units are involved. Example: marketing people might think a requirement is a must-have because they feel it will help sell the product, while IT people might think it is a won’t-have or at most a could-have. If the suggested requirement can add significant value to the product, its priority might be raised, resulting in a de-scoping or recategorization of other items to compensate. In extreme cases this might lead to the elimination of requirements like refactoring and maintenance in IT that, in retrospect, could prove vital for the success of the product."},
  {"tag": "2.2", "question": "How are MoSCoW and Kano combined (Figure 12), what does the combination offer, and what mix is recommended in iterative projects?", "answer": "To avoid MoSCoW’s limitations, combine its simplicity with the customer-centric view of Kano. Must-have items populate the basic category, as they are necessary for the functionality customers expect. Should-haves boost customer satisfaction, so they are typically placed on the satisfier line and occasionally in the delighter category, along with the could-haves and won’t-haves. This combined view offers greater insight into the requirements and supports more informed decision-making about their priorities. In an iterative approach, have a good mix of should-haves and could-haves along with must-haves, to ensure basic expectations are met while continuing to improve linear satisfiers and add some delighters when possible. As the project progresses the mix varies, and if time and resources allow, a shift toward the delighters might be considered. Figure 12: the Kano chart with M, S, C, W letters placed on the curves (C and W only on the delighter curve). Figure detail: M markers appear on all three curves; for the exam use the text (must-haves = basic). (tutor-only, CONFLICT) The tutor said won’t-haves have no Kano equivalent; the book places them in the delighter category."},
  {"tag": "2.2", "question": "How can the threat of subjectivity in prioritization be avoided? Include the examples, the multi-project approach, and what team members need to know.", "answer": "Mnemonic ‘Past, People, Practices’: careful consideration of past project failures, understanding dysfunctional teams, and adopting standard practices to account for uncertainties. Examples of such practices: adding neutral periods (buffers) between activities and making range estimates for high-priority activities, so that disruptions to project delivery can be avoided; a document review period can be such an activity. When multiple projects are involved, a portfolio approach might be adopted that organizes projects into programs and subprograms (Patanakul, 2015). Team members need to be aware of the project in its entirety, not just their part, so they can proactively identify areas that might impact their work and how their work will impact other areas."},
  {"tag": "2.2", "question": "LONG ANSWER (18 points): Explain why and how requirements are prioritized in IT projects. Discuss urgency vs importance and the matrix, the Kano model, MoSCoW, their combination, and how subjectivity can be reduced.", "answer": "Why: prioritization decides the order of work, based on risk, funding, strategic objectives and other ongoing projects; it organizes the sequence of work, identifies milestones, allows monitoring and alerts to task breakdowns and priority shifts; high-priority activities draw the team’s attention and receive resources to finish on time and on budget (Larson & Gray). Tasks are prioritized by urgency or importance. Urgent activities/crises need immediate attention; reactive, defensive, narrowly focused mindset; knee-jerk responses may jeopardize success. Important activities ensure long-term goals; proactive mode, rational decisions, openness to opportunities, may deter future crises, but suffer from subjective evaluation (e.g. sponsor’s or PM’s career). Matrix (Covey; Eisenhower): urgent = high priority; important = ongoing attention, can be delayed. Critical (high/high, Focus, top priority, crises); Goals (high importance/low urgency, Attend: planning, opportunities, collaborations, development); Interruptions (low importance/high urgency, Avoid: e.g. reporting progress to external stakeholders); Distractions (low/low, Limit: waste of time and resources, e.g. overanalyzing, micromanaging, gossiping). Kano (Lo et al.; quality control and customer satisfaction): challenges the belief that satisfaction is proportional to improving every aspect; classifies requirements into categories via classifiers (3–5 categories). Basic: obvious minimum, unmentioned, met at any cost, quality tested (mobility), high and mandatory priority. Linear satisfiers/performance: proportional satisfaction, standard functionality (response speed), ROI eventually drops, lower priority but implement. Delighters: unexpected, ‘blow the mind’, premium (human-like head movement), may distract, become basic when overdone, impact declines; implement a few. MoSCoW (Dai Clengg, Oracle UK, 1994): words widen categories and trigger discussion; must (skeleton), should (polished surface), could (face drawn on chassis), won’t; problem when different organizational units disagree (marketing vs IT), causing de-scoping and possibly eliminating refactoring and maintenance. Combination: must = basic; should = satisfier line, occasionally delighter; could and won’t = delighter; greater insight and informed decisions; in iterative projects a good mix of should- and could-haves with must-haves, shifting toward delighters as time and resources allow. Subjectivity reduced by considering past project failures, understanding dysfunctional teams, and standard practices for uncertainty (buffers, range estimates, document review periods); portfolio approach (programs, subprograms) for multiple projects; team members aware of the whole project."},
  
  {"tag": "2.3", "question": "What is the comprehensive project management plan, what does it define, and what role does it play once the project is running?", "answer": "All subsidiary plans have to be defined, prepared and assembled into a comprehensive project management plan (an integrated plan). It defines how the project is executed, monitored and controlled, when deliverables will be submitted, and when the product or service will be delivered to the client (Papke-Shields & Boyer-Wright, 2017). Once the project is running, these plans serve as a basis for monitoring and controlling the project, helping to ensure the expected results are delivered on time and on budget. A prioritized and agreed-upon set of requirements gives clarity of what is expected, alongside the project objectives and constraints, before the actual planning of activities starts."},
  {"tag": "2.3", "question": "What uncertainties must planning contend with, what are the agile and the opposing arguments about detailed planning, and which approach suits which projects?", "answer": "Planning must contend with many uncertainties due to changing environments, changing requirements, work taking longer than expected, etc. Agile proponents: planning work in detail is not adequate because it makes it difficult to adapt to changes that will become necessary anyway; plan only on a high level and add detail at a very late stage, typically at the start of a new short iteration. Opposing view: without detailed planning, dependencies between work packages are easily overlooked and important properties such as safety and security may be implemented inconsistently. Projects decide their level of detail depending on size, type of project and organizational culture. Plan-driven (classical) approach: particularly suitable for large projects, especially in regulated environments (finance, safety, security). Agile approach: particularly suitable for small projects in a fast-moving environment. In practice most projects are somewhere in between and need an adequate mix of methods."},
  {"tag": "2.3", "question": "Which tools and techniques are used for sequencing activities, and which is the most popular?", "answer": "Sequencing activities in time is probably the most important aspect of any planning. Tools (mnemonic ‘Please Pass Light Gravy’): Precedence diagramming, Program Evaluation Review Technique (PERT), Leads and lags, Gantt charts. Gantt charts are the most popular technique and are often required in formal project proposals. The other methods are not always required, but they provide valuable insights and support the development of an accurate Gantt chart."},
  {"tag": "2.3", "question": "What is precedence diagramming, and what are the four dependency types with the cRobot examples (Figure 13)? Include the SF inconsistency.", "answer": "Precedence diagramming organizes activities in pairs as nodes graphically linked by logical relationships. Since activities have a beginning and an end, there are four ways to pair a predecessor and successor. FS (finish-to-start): the successor starts after the end of its predecessor; cRobot: testing cannot commence until the corresponding robot components are completed. SS (start-to-start): the successor starts between the predecessor’s beginning and end; cRobot: software components can begin development as soon as the hardware is finalized and in construction, not necessarily after assembly is completed. SF (start-to-finish), book version: the successor can finish before the predecessor; cRobot: much of the software will be completed before the robot body is complete. FF (finish-to-finish): the successor finishes after the predecessor; cRobot: certain unit tests must be done before a complete system test. Figure 13 shows the four as bar pairs, with a lead arrow on FF and a lag arrow on SS. INCONSISTENCY (exam vs correct version): the tutor corrected SF to the standard meaning, ‘the successor cannot finish before the predecessor starts’ (reverse logic, rare). In MCQs use the book’s wording; in essays give the book’s version, then the clarification. (tutor-only) FS is the most common and is the default when no dependency type is given."},
  {"tag": "2.3", "question": "MCQ: In the cRobot example, testing activities cannot commence until the corresponding robot components are completed. Which dependency type is this?\nA) Start-to-start\nB) Finish-to-start\nC) Finish-to-finish\nD) Start-to-finish", "answer": "B. Finish-to-start (FS): the successor starts after the end of its predecessor."},
  {"tag": "2.3", "question": "Define lead time, lag time and slack (float). How do activity priorities affect the choice of lead or lag?", "answer": "When two interdependent activities overlap (as in FF and SS): lead time = the closest we can move the successor to the predecessor’s beginning; lag time = the furthest we can move the successor’s beginning from the predecessor’s beginning. Both are typically measured in days or weeks. The priorities of each activity determine the lead or lag adopted: a high-priority successor should start as early as possible to have more time for unexpected disturbances; a resource-intensive activity may be delayed as much as possible (increasing the lag) to take advantage of resources released from other activities. Slack or float = the maximum variability we have in the commencement of an activity."},
  {"tag": "2.3", "question": "Describe the hypothetical project case (Figure 14): its contents, the difference between deliverables and milestones, and the figure errors.", "answer": "A project breakdown with four work packages, their tasks/activities, duration (weeks), dependency on previous tasks, deliverables and milestones. Deliverables = the physical products of the project (reports included) that must be reviewed and accepted. Milestones = moments in time indicating significant accomplishments and transitions in project work. Table: 1.1 (3, –, D1.1); 1.2 (4, 1.1); 1.3 (3, 1.2, M1); 2.1 (10, 1.2); 2.2 (8, 2.1, M2); 2.3 (4, 2.1); 2.4 (6, 2.1); 3.1 (8, 1.3+2.2+2.3+2.4, M3); 3.2 (5, 3.1); 3.3 (5, 3.1); 3.4 (4, dependency blank); 4.1 (2, 3.3); 4.2 (4, 3.4+4.1, M4). Each task Tx.y has deliverable Dx.y. FIGURE ERRORS: Figure 14 is titled ‘Relation Types in Precedence Diagrams’ although it shows the task table; 3.4’s dependency is blank, while the PERT chart shows 3.2 → 3.4."},
  {"tag": "2.3", "question": "What is PERT, what does a PERT node contain (Figure 15), how is a PERT chart built, and what does the critical path mean?", "answer": "The Program Evaluation Review Technique (PERT) organizes project tasks in an event-oriented fashion and estimates the minimum time needed as well as the longest stretch of dependent activities (the critical path) required to complete the project. Each task/activity is a node containing the task identifier, its duration, the earliest and latest times work can start, and the earliest and latest times it could finish. Figure 15 (PERT element): circle with top row earliest start time | earliest finish time; middle row task ID | duration; bottom row latest start time | latest finish time. Building it: create a node for the first activity, add subsequent nodes for each task, and connect nodes with arrows from predecessor to successor until the last task is reached. Red arrows (typically) highlight the critical path: the activities with zero flexibility in starting and ending times. A delay of a task on the critical path will always delay overall project completion."},
  {"tag": "2.3", "question": "Explain the forward pass, backward pass and slack calculation, including the rules for multiple predecessors and successors. Note the slack wording error.", "answer": "Forward pass (beginning to end, earliest times): default start time is day zero; earliest finish time EFT = earliest start time EST + duration; this EFT becomes the EST of the next activity. With multiple predecessors, the maximum/latest finish time becomes the successor’s start, because all predecessors must be completed first. Backward pass (end to beginning, latest times): start at the project end; latest start time LST = latest finish time LFT − duration; this LST becomes the latest the predecessor can finish. With multiple successors, take the minimum/earliest time. Slack = latest − earliest (LST − EST, or LFT − EFT). Activities with no slack cannot vary their start and end times and form the critical path. ERROR (exam vs correct version): the book says slack is calculated by ‘subtracting the maximum latest ending times from the corresponding earliest ones’, which is reversed and gives a negative number; correct: latest minus earliest."},
  {"tag": "2.3", "question": "Work through the coursebook’s critical path example (Figures 16–18): forward pass, backward pass, slack, critical path and project duration.", "answer": "Format: task (duration): EST–EFT | LST–LFT | slack.\n1.1 (3): 0–3 | 0–3 | 0\n1.2 (4): 3–7 | 3–7 | 0\n1.3 (3): 7–10 | 22–25 | 15\n2.1 (10): 7–17 | 7–17 | 0\n2.2 (8): 17–25 | 17–25 | 0\n2.3 (4): 17–21 | 21–25 | 4\n2.4 (6): 17–23 | 19–25 | 2\n3.1 (8): EST = max(10, 25, 21, 23) = 25 → 25–33 | 25–33 | 0\n3.2 (5): 33–38 | 33–38 | 0\n3.3 (5): 33–38 | 35–40 | 2\n3.4 (4): 38–42 | 38–42 | 0\n4.1 (2): 38–40 | 40–42 | 2\n4.2 (4): EST = max(42, 40) = 42 → 42–46 | 42–46 | 0\nBackward-pass minimum rules: 3.1 LFT = min(33, 35) = 33; 2.1 LFT = min(17, 21, 19) = 17; 1.2 LFT = min(22, 7) = 7.\nCritical path: 1.1 → 1.2 → 2.1 → 2.2 → 3.1 → 3.2 → 3.4 → 4.2 = 3+4+10+8+8+5+4+4 = 46 weeks. The earliest the project can be completed is 46 weeks."},
  {"tag": "2.3", "question": "PRACTICE (exam-style MCQ, all dependencies finish-to-start): A (3 days, –), B (4, A), C (2, A), D (5, B and C), E (6, C). Project end requires D and E. What is the critical path and project duration?\nA) A–C–E, 11 days\nB) A–B–D, 12 days\nC) A–C–D, 10 days\nD) A–B–D, 14 days", "answer": "B. Paths: A–B–D = 3+4+5 = 12; A–C–D = 3+2+5 = 10; A–C–E = 3+2+6 = 11. The longest path is the critical path: A–B–D, 12 days. Forward pass check: A 0–3; B 3–7; C 3–5; D starts max(7,5)=7 → 12; E 5–11; project end = max(12, 11) = 12. Slack: C = 2 (on path to D) / E path slack 1. (tutor-only) In the exam, expect 4–6 tasks with FS by default; it is worth one MCQ point, so do it last and don’t spend more than a minute."},
  {"tag": "2.3", "question": "What does Figure 19 show, and what is the inconsistency in its title?", "answer": "Figure 19 shows the activities as bars along a time axis (0, 3, 7, 17, 25, 33, 38, 42, 46) with their earliest and latest start and end times. Red bars (no slack) are the critical path: 1.1, 1.2, 2.1, 2.2, 3.1, 3.2, 3.4, 4.2. Other bars show their slack: 1.3 slack 15, 2.3 slack 4, 2.4 slack 2, 3.3/4.1 slack 2. Activities with no slack cannot vary their start and end times and form the critical path. TITLE ERROR: Figure 19 is titled ‘Relation Types in Precedence Diagrams’ (same as Figures 13 and 14), although it shows slack times on a timeline."},
  {"tag": "2.3", "question": "What is a Gantt chart (Figure 20), what is its advantage, and what are its limitations? How can tool limitations be countered?", "answer": "A Gantt chart is a bar chart that illustrates the project schedule. Dependency relationships are indicated by arrows connecting predecessors to successors; project status is indicated by dark blue bars. Figure 20: WPs 1–4 and tasks 1.1–4.2 against weeks 1–46. Advantage: it allows the work to be visualized, which can reveal a project’s complexity and help in setting realistic time frames for completing activities. Limitations (mnemonic CRR): Complex: can become very complex, especially in large projects with multiple interdependent tasks; Recording: not static, progress needs to be constantly recorded; Resources: they do not reveal the amount of resources required to complete tasks and their costs (e.g. no way to differentiate an eight-week task needing 20 people from one needing 10). To counter the limitations of a tool, multiple techniques can be applied, e.g. considering both PERT and Gantt charts."},
  {"tag": "2.3", "question": "MCQ (IU unit test): What is a disadvantage of using Gantt charts?\nA) They display the dependencies of various project activities.\nB) They indicate the amount of time it will take to complete the project.\nC) They provide a visual display of project progress.\nD) They do not reveal the amount of resources that are required to complete tasks.", "answer": "D (confirmed IU key). A, B and C are features or advantages of Gantt charts."},
  {"tag": "2.3", "question": "Which additional plans are developed in large projects, what signifies the end of the planning phase, and is planning then over?", "answer": "Additional plans are often developed (especially in large projects) for managing scope, requirements, cost, quality, process improvement, human resources, communications, risk, procurement, and stakeholders. Approval of the developed plans by the project stakeholders signifies the end of the planning phase and the move to the execution phase. Planning is not over: depending on the domain and the adopted life cycle approach, the plan might be revised and modified as new evidence from project execution and monitoring shows deviations from the requirements and expected outcome."},
  {"tag": "2.3", "question": "(tutor-only) What does the tutor add about critical paths, hard/soft logic and schedule recovery?", "answer": "(tutor-only) The critical path is the path with the longest duration, which defines the total project duration; every project has one, and a project can have more than one critical path, which makes it riskier because all of them must be monitored. Hard logic: a dependency that cannot be negotiated, usually physical (e.g. you can’t build a wall before the foundation). Soft logic: a sequence that could be run in parallel (e.g. testing and writing the user manual). Recovery techniques for a delayed critical path (the tutor said ‘not relevant for our course’): crashing (adding people, up to a saturation point), resource levelling (moving idle resources), fast tracking (running tasks in parallel where there is no dependency)."},
  {"tag": "2.3", "question": "LONG ANSWER (18 points): Explain how project activities are scheduled. Cover the integrated plan and the planning debate, the sequencing tools, dependency types, lead/lag/slack, PERT and the critical path calculation, and Gantt charts with their limitations.", "answer": "Integrated plan: all subsidiary plans are assembled into a comprehensive project management plan defining how the project is executed, monitored and controlled and when deliverables and the product are delivered; it is the basis for monitoring and control (on time, on budget). Planning must contend with uncertainty (changing environments and requirements, work taking longer). Agile: detailed planning hinders adaptation, so plan high-level and detail at the start of each short iteration; opponents: without detail, dependencies are overlooked and safety/security implemented inconsistently. Detail depends on size, type and culture; plan-driven suits large and regulated projects (finance, safety, security), agile suits small fast-moving ones; most projects mix. Sequencing is the most important aspect of planning. Tools: precedence diagramming, PERT, leads and lags, Gantt (most popular, often required in proposals; the others support an accurate Gantt). Precedence diagramming links activities as nodes: FS (successor starts after predecessor ends; testing after components), SS (starts between predecessor’s start and end; software once hardware is in construction), SF (book: successor can finish before predecessor; software before robot body; standard: cannot finish before predecessor starts), FF (finishes after predecessor; unit tests before system test). Lead time = closest the successor can move to the predecessor’s beginning; lag time = furthest; priorities decide (high-priority successors early, resource-intensive activities delayed); slack/float = maximum variability in an activity’s commencement. Example: 4 WPs with tasks, durations, dependencies, deliverables (reviewed and accepted) and milestones (significant accomplishments and transitions). PERT: event-oriented; estimates minimum time and the critical path (longest stretch of dependent activities); nodes show task ID, duration, earliest and latest start and finish; arrows predecessor → successor; red arrows = critical path, zero flexibility; delay there delays the project. Forward pass: day 0, EFT = EST + duration, maximum of predecessors. Backward pass: LST = LFT − duration, minimum of successors. Slack = latest − earliest; zero-slack activities form the critical path (example: 1.1–1.2–2.1–2.2–3.1–3.2–3.4–4.2 = 46 weeks). Gantt: bar chart of the schedule with dependency arrows and status bars; visualizes work, reveals complexity, helps set realistic time frames; limitations: very complex in large projects, progress must be constantly recorded, no resources or costs shown (20 vs 10 people). Counter limitations by combining PERT and Gantt. Additional plans (scope, requirements, cost, quality, process improvement, HR, communications, risk, procurement, stakeholders); stakeholder approval ends planning and starts execution, but plans are revised as execution and monitoring reveal deviations."},

  {"tag": "2.4", "question": "What forms can a deliverable take, what does acceptance mean, and what levels of acceptance should projects define?", "answer": "The output of project activity is typically a deliverable, either a physical entity/object or a document (in the case of requirements, services, or decisions/conclusions drawn during the activity). A deliverable needs to be accepted by the project stakeholders or their proxies. Acceptance means that the criteria established as requirements for the outcome of the work are met in the deliverable the activity produced. Projects should usually define separate levels of acceptance, from that required for individual minor changes to that required for the overall project result; different techniques and methods are commonly used for this step."},
  {"tag": "2.4", "question": "What is the Definition of Done (DoD): what does ‘done’ generally mean, what does the DoD ensure, and who defines it? Give the glossary definition.", "answer": "In IT and in agile software development in particular, a popular acceptance concept is the Definition of Done (DoD) (Silva et al., 2017). There is no generic consensus on what ‘done’ means, but it is generally considered to mean that the product/software is (mnemonic ‘Can Robots Test In Daylight’): coded as per the agreed-upon standards, reviewed, units tested, fully integrated, and documented. The DoD ensures quality and transparency and that everyone on the development team knows exactly what each deliverable is. In agile development it is usually defined by the development team itself and serves as an internal quality assurance mechanism before the results are passed on to any outside stakeholders. Glossary: ‘a previously agreed upon set of criteria, or definition, concerning a task that, when completed, is considered to be finished.’ (tutor-only) In waterfall the equivalent is called acceptance criteria; in agile, ‘done’. It proves completion, so the customer cannot keep claiming the work is unfinished."},
  {"tag": "2.4", "question": "How and why does the DoD vary and evolve, how are its benefits ensured, what is the common misconception, and who initiates it?", "answer": "Variations: because team constitution often varies, details specific to the solution are revealed; variations might require that the code be peer-reviewed, be operational in a test environment where various categories of tests are passed, be well documented, and that the user interface be accepted by the customer and approved by the product owner. Evolution: as the team bonds and becomes more cohesive, shared perspectives form as they recognize patterns in processes and procedures that result in high-value deliverables (Karabulut & Ergun, 2018); the team then revisits its DoD to leverage its expertise and common understanding of the final outcome. Ensuring benefits: everyone must be aware of it, and it must be regularly revisited and discussed. Misconception: the DoD is about the final approval of the project outcome; not true, DoDs are mainly adopted for quality purposes and for inter-team and multi-team consumption. It is initiated and supported by the team, not by any external party; it cannot be imposed but must be collectively adopted."},
  {"tag": "2.4", "question": "MCQ: Which statement about the Definition of Done is FALSE according to the coursebook?\nA) It is usually defined by the development team itself.\nB) It is mainly about the final approval of the project outcome.\nC) It ensures quality and transparency.\nD) It cannot be imposed but must be collectively adopted.", "answer": "B. This is the misconception named in the coursebook: DoDs are mainly adopted for quality purposes and for inter-team and multi-team consumption, not for final approval."},
  {"tag": "2.4", "question": "Which standards do DoDs draw on? Name the ISO family and the specific standard, and what the quality in use model represents.", "answer": "DoDs seem arbitrary (project- and team-specific), but they make use of industry-established quality standards and processes developed over years of experience, especially in large and more streamlined project types. One such tool is ISO/IEC 25000, System and Software Quality Requirements and Evaluation (SQuaRE) family of standards. Of specific interest is ISO/IEC 25010:2011, which focuses on system and software quality models (Haoues, Sellami, Ben-Abdallah, & Cheikhi, 2017). It defines a quality in use model that determines which quality characteristics are taken into account when evaluating the properties of an IT (in particular software) product. It represents stakeholder needs such as functionality, performance, security, attainability, etc., addressed through five categories. (tutor-only, ERROR) The tutor called ISO 25000 ‘the standard for project management’; it is a software quality standard."},
  {"tag": "2.4", "question": "Name and define the five categories of the ISO/IEC 25010 quality in use model, with their subcategories.", "answer": "Mnemonics: SCEEF, or ‘Some Elephants Fly Every Christmas’. 1) Satisfaction: the degree to which user needs are satisfied when a product or system is used in a specified context of use; a combination of usefulness (degree to which a user is satisfied with their perceived achievement of pragmatic goals, including the results and consequences of use), trust (degree to which a user or other stakeholder has confidence that the product or system will behave as intended), pleasure (degree to which a user obtains pleasure from fulfilling their personal needs), and comfort (degree to which the user is satisfied with physical comfort) (‘Unicorns Trust Pink Clouds’). 2) Context coverage: the degree to which a product or system can be used with effectiveness, efficiency, freedom from risk, and satisfaction in both specified contexts of use and in contexts beyond those initially explicitly identified; aspects: context completeness (usable in all the specified contexts of use, e.g. on a small screen, with low network bandwidth, by a nonexpert user, in a fault-tolerant mode such as no network connectivity) and flexibility (usable in contexts beyond those initially specified in the requirements; takes account of circumstances, opportunities and individual preferences not anticipated in advance). 3) Effectiveness: the accuracy and completeness with which users achieve specified goals. 4) Efficiency: the ratio with which resources are expended in relation to the accuracy and completeness with which users achieve goals. 5) Freedom from risk: the degree to which a product or system mitigates the potential risk to economic status, human life, health, or the environment; includes economic risk mitigation (financial status, efficient operation, commercial property, reputation or other resources), health and safety risk mitigation (risk to people), and environmental risk mitigation (risk to property or the environment), all in the intended contexts of use."},
  {"tag": "2.4", "question": "MCQ: Which ISO/IEC 25010 quality in use category is defined as ‘the ratio with which resources are expended in relation to the accuracy and completeness with which users achieve goals’?\nA) Effectiveness\nB) Efficiency\nC) Satisfaction\nD) Context coverage", "answer": "B. Efficiency. Effectiveness is only the accuracy and completeness of achieving specified goals (no resource ratio)."},
  {"tag": "2.4", "question": "What does the ISO/IEC 25010 product quality model relate to, and what are its eight characteristics?", "answer": "The product quality model relates to the static properties of software and the dynamic properties of the computer system. It is composed of eight quality characteristics and their subcategories. Mnemonic SCRUMPPEFS: Security, Compatibility, Reliability, Usability, Maintainability, Portability, Performance Efficiency, Functional Suitability. (tutor-only) If you can only remember some of the eight, write those; points are given per correct item."},
  {"tag": "2.4", "question": "Define Functional suitability, Performance efficiency and Usability (ISO/IEC 25010 product quality) and list their elements. Note the slide error.", "answer": "Functional suitability: the degree to which a product or system provides functions that meet stated and implied needs when used under specified conditions. Elements: functional completeness (the set of functions covers all specified tasks and user objectives), functional correctness (provides correct results with the needed degree of precision), functional appropriateness (functions facilitate accomplishing specified tasks and objectives; e.g. the user is only presented with the necessary steps to complete a task). Performance efficiency: performance relative to the amount of resources used under stated conditions. Aspects: time behavior (response and processing times and throughput rates meet requirements) and capacity (maximum limits of a parameter meet requirements). Usability: the degree to which a product or system can be used by specified users to achieve specified goals with effectiveness, efficiency and satisfaction in a specified context of use. Elements: appropriateness recognizability (users can recognize whether it is appropriate for their needs), learnability (specified users can learn to use it with effectiveness, efficiency, freedom from risk and satisfaction), operability (attributes make it easy to operate and control), user error protection (protects users against making errors), user interface aesthetics (pleasing and satisfying interaction), accessibility (usable by people with the widest range of characteristics and capabilities). SLIDE ERROR: the slide splits ‘appropriateness recognizability’ into two items; it is one element, so usability has six elements."},
  {"tag": "2.4", "question": "Define Reliability, Security and Compatibility (ISO/IEC 25010 product quality) and list their elements.", "answer": "Reliability: the degree to which a system, product or component performs specified functions under specified conditions for a specified period of time. Aspects: maturity (meets needs for reliability under normal operation), availability (operational and accessible when required for use), fault tolerance (operates as intended despite hardware or software faults), recoverability (after an interruption or failure, can recover the data directly affected and reestablish the desired state). Security: the degree to which a product or system protects information and data so that persons or other products or systems have the degree of data access appropriate to their types and levels of authorization. Elements: confidentiality (data accessible only to those authorized), integrity (prevents unauthorized access to, or modification of, programs or data), non-repudiation (actions or events can be proven to have taken place, so they cannot be repudiated later), accountability (actions of an entity can be traced uniquely to that entity), authenticity (the identity of a subject or resource can be proved to be the one claimed). Compatibility: the ability for two or more systems to work together without any alterations made to enable this. Elements: coexistence (performs its functions efficiently while sharing a common environment and resources with other products, without detrimental impact on them) and interoperability (two or more systems, products or components can exchange information and use it)."},
  {"tag": "2.4", "question": "Define Portability and Maintainability (ISO/IEC 25010 product quality) and list their elements.", "answer": "Portability: the degree of effectiveness and efficiency with which a system, product or component can be transferred from one hardware, software, or other operational or usage environment to another. Aspects: adaptability (can be effectively and efficiently adapted for different or evolving hardware, software or other environments), installability (can be successfully installed and/or uninstalled in a specified environment), replaceability (can replace another specified software product for the same purpose in the same environment). Maintainability: the degree of effectiveness and efficiency with which a product or system can be modified by the intended maintainers. Elements: modularity (composed of discrete components so a change to one has minimal impact on others), reusability (an asset can be used in more than one system or in building other assets), analyzability (it is possible to assess the impact of an intended change, diagnose deficiencies or causes of failures, or identify parts to be modified), modifiability (can be modified without introducing defects or degrading existing quality), testability (test criteria can be established and tests performed to determine whether they are met)."},
  {"tag": "2.4", "question": "MCQ: Confidentiality, integrity, non-repudiation, accountability and authenticity are elements of which ISO/IEC 25010 product quality characteristic?\nA) Reliability\nB) Maintainability\nC) Security\nD) Compatibility", "answer": "C. Security. Reliability = maturity, availability, fault tolerance, recoverability; Maintainability = modularity, reusability, analyzability, modifiability, testability; Compatibility = coexistence, interoperability."},
  {"tag": "2.4", "question": "What is the purpose of standards (and what is it NOT), why does shared understanding matter, and what are their benefits and drawbacks?", "answer": "The list of quality attributes showcases the complexity and multiple aspects of quality in IT. The purpose of standards is not for guiding project work but for emphasizing communication and a shared understanding of what is expected of the final outcome (Rehacek, 2017). This matters for large teams distributed around the world and for small groups sharing the same office, because it lets teams focus on what is important instead of each member’s subjective view of importance. Benefits (mnemonic ‘Best Managers Attract Partners’): they encapsulate best practices; improve the management process; become a practice the organization can promote to attract customers and meet the compliance requirements of existing customers; their adoption is viewed positively when cooperation with external entities (partners, government, etc.) becomes necessary. Drawbacks: often perceived as costly, documentation-intensive and bureaucratic; organizations sometimes find it difficult to relate standards to their business needs and justify their application."},
  {"tag": "2.4", "question": "What does acceptance of the final product typically involve, where are the procedures stated, and what details must be agreed?", "answer": "A clear and widely accepted DoD and set of quality standards can be great contributors to success, as long as the final product gets accepted by both internal and external stakeholders (Jeong, Bae, & Jeong, 2015). Acceptance typically signifies that the project elements, and specifically the final solution, have been tested in an enumerated procedure that defines the minimum requirements for the project to be completed. Such tests and/or acceptance procedures are typically stated in the contract agreements between the customer and the project team/organization. Acceptance procedures and steps can be divided into several levels and among stakeholders. Details include: the presence or absence of the customer; the location of the test (typically done at both provider and customer sites, often under real operational conditions); and what happens if the solution does not pass any or some of the agreed-upon tests."},
  {"tag": "2.4", "question": "Why might acceptance not be straightforward, and what does the coursebook suggest instead? Name the domains of the multi-domain framework.", "answer": "Performance measures can fail for a variety of reasons (mnemonic SMP): Subjective evaluation metrics (e.g. how the user interface feels) not balanced by objective metrics (e.g. speed of execution of a function); Many measures implemented at the same time without coordination, leading to incorrect measurements and rushed evaluations by management; Politics: organizational politics can slant performance measurements, assign the wrong people to be in charge of acceptance tests, and downplay performance metrics. Instead, a wider stakeholder perspective at different points in time throughout the project life cycle and after project closeout should be considered when selecting acceptance criteria. A ‘multi-domain framework’ for measuring IS/IT project success (Bannerman & Thorogood, 2012) could include independent domains (mnemonic ‘Pretty Purple Parrots Bite Snakes’): process, project management, product, business, and strategy, to allow a breakdown of a project’s success or failure."},
  {"tag": "2.4", "question": "What is a quality gate? Give the text and glossary definitions.", "answer": "An alternative form of intermediate acceptance of results that is more common in large projects. A quality gate consists of a definition of results to be delivered at a certain point in the project, such as at the end of a project stage, plus some form of formal review to check that all expected results are available in adequate quality (Kneuper, 2018, p. 26). Glossary: ‘more general than a milestone, a quality gate is a form of quality check at certain stages of a project.’"},
  {"tag": "2.4", "question": "Unit 2 summary: what does it say, and which inconsistency must you avoid in the exam?", "answer": "Summary: a WBS divides the overall work into smaller packages, usually on multiple levels, but eventually only the results produced (deliverables, defined in advance) matter; an important deliverable may be assigned a date, which leads to a milestone. Graphical representations of the work and its dependencies follow; the most widely used are the Gantt chart and the PERT chart. Agile projects break down work into epics, user stories and tasks collected in a product backlog; tasks must be small enough for one iteration or sprint. The DoD is usually agreed within the team and defines the conditions for an activity to be declared ‘done’. The traceability matrix relates the initial requirements to intermediate results, deliverables and work packages, ensuring all requirements are addressed at all relevant levels and no additional requirements emerge that were not defined. Since there is always more work than possible, prioritization is an important PM task. INCONSISTENCY: the summary lists MoSCoW as ‘Must do, Should do, Could do, and Won’t do’; section 2.2 says must-haves, should-haves, could-haves, won’t-haves. In the IU unit test, ‘must-do, should-do, could-do, won’t-do’ was a WRONG option. Always use the ‘-haves’."},
  {"tag": "2.4", "question": "LONG ANSWER (18 points): Explain how deliverables are accepted in IT projects. Discuss the Definition of Done, the ISO/IEC 25010 quality models, the purpose and pros and cons of standards, acceptance procedures and their difficulties, and quality gates.", "answer": "Deliverables (physical objects or documents) must be accepted by stakeholders or proxies; acceptance = the requirement criteria are met in the deliverable; projects define separate levels of acceptance (minor changes to overall result). DoD: popular in IT and agile (Silva et al.); generally coded to agreed standards, reviewed, unit tested, fully integrated, documented; ensures quality, transparency, shared knowledge of each deliverable; defined by the development team as an internal QA mechanism before results go to outside stakeholders. It varies (peer review, test environment passing test categories, documentation, UI accepted by customer and approved by product owner) and evolves as the team becomes cohesive (Karabulut & Ergun); everyone must know it and revisit it; not about final approval but quality and inter-/multi-team consumption; initiated by the team, collectively adopted, not imposed. Standards: ISO/IEC 25000 SQuaRE; ISO/IEC 25010:2011 system and software quality models. Quality in use model (stakeholder needs): satisfaction (usefulness, trust, pleasure, comfort), effectiveness (accuracy and completeness of achieving goals), freedom from risk (economic, health and safety, environmental), efficiency (resources relative to accuracy and completeness), context coverage (context completeness, flexibility). Product quality model (static software and dynamic system properties): functional suitability, performance efficiency, usability, reliability, security, compatibility, portability, maintainability, each with elements. Standards are not for guiding work but for communication and shared understanding of the expected outcome (Rehacek); benefits: best practices, better management process, attracting customers and compliance, positive for external cooperation; drawbacks: costly, documentation-intensive, bureaucratic, hard to justify. Acceptance (Jeong et al.): tested in an enumerated procedure of minimum requirements, stated in the contract, divided into levels and among stakeholders; agree customer presence, test location (provider and customer sites, real conditions), and consequences of failing. Difficulties: subjective vs objective metrics, too many uncoordinated measures, organizational politics; remedy: wider stakeholder perspective over time and after closeout, multi-domain framework (process, project management, product, business, strategy; Bannerman & Thorogood). Quality gate: intermediate acceptance in large projects; definition of results at a point (e.g. stage end) plus formal review of adequate quality (Kneuper); more general than a milestone."}

]
